<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Bructeri</h1><p class="st-meta">12 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Germanic people of Westphalia who fought Rome alongside Arminius and sheltered the seeress Veleda during the Batavian revolt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bructeri lived between the upper Ems and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lippe-river">Lippe</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. They appear in Roman sources from 12 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus">Drusus</a> fought them on the Ems. In 9 CE they joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a> and captured the eagle of the Nineteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>' general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-stertinius">Stertinius</a> recovered in 15.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their leadership was in the hands of chiefs and an influential religious figure: the seeress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veleda">Veleda</a>, who lived in a tower near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lippe-river">Lippe</a>, foretold the success of the Batavian revolt of 69 and was consulted by other peoples. Around 98, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> reports that neighbouring tribes had nearly wiped out the Bructeri while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> watched.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Bructeri survived that defeat and are mentioned again in the 4th century as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a>. Their district, the Boructuari, was among the peoples evangelised by Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suitbert">Suitbert</a> around 700, before the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a> overran it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bructeri.jpg" alt="Bructeri" width="320" height="162"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Lancaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Rhine_10BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>12 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Weser-Rhine Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=144&amp;focus=bructeri" aria-label="Bructeri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4906 418 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bructeri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5226 674h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5226 674h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=144&amp;focus=bructeri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
